    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.icons\n\n### Description\n\nI am using `mini.icons` in a way that sometimes icons are presented in a dark background, and sometimes in a light one.\nBecause the global style (dark or light), as far as I know, is unique, one of those two instances look bad:\n\nGood looking on `mini.pick` dark background:\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/11538880-af0c-4492-b3c0-903fcf776aa5)\n\nBad looking on top winbar with inverted colors\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0c36d8ef-47a9-42f6-a0d0-d7633219a833)\n\nThe top winbar icon gets better with `background = light` but the colorscheme gets messed up:\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3b6100a5-432c-4b3a-b541-1aa1094b36e3)\n\n### Question\nIs there a way to improve this behaviour? Can I manually get an icon with the corresponding inverted highlight group? That way, I could put it wherever I want to use a inverted background.",
    "closed_at": "2025-02-07T08:31:46Z",
