            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Yes, this is possible by using custom \"inverted\" groups.\n\nThe 'mini.icons' module by design uses only a [handful of highlight groups](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3049adbafd08b769d0700624fe9b85fa4176c88c/doc/mini-icons.txt#L155-L163). It means that it is quite feasible to both define in a color scheme and manage them on your own.\n\nSo this use case can be done something like this:\n- Define custom `MiniIconsXxxInv` highlight groups that set foreground to a color with bigger lightness. Exact colors depend on the color scheme or can be computed with helpers from 'mini.colors' (convert to  OKLch color space, set high lightness, convert back to HEX color).\n- Use *those* highlight groups in contexts that require a brighter color. It might be something like `local icon, hl = MiniIcons.get(...)` followed by `local actual_hl = hl .. 'Inv'`. The exact way to \"hook\" that depends on the context/plugin and might require changes in the plugin's source code.\n\nA more straightforward approach might be to trade the \"use brighter color for brighter background\" requirement for the \"use no color for brighter background\". That way it always gets highlighted in the same way as in the first screenshot.\n\nHope this helps.",
